Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns & their potential usage
• "refresh_date": find the most‑recent extraction of the weekly files  
• "week": identify the calendar week that the trends correspond to and calculate “one year prior”  
• "rank": pick the top‑ranked (=1) rising term for a given week  
• "term": the actual search phrase we have to return in the end  
• "percent_gain": another measure of “rising”, helpful when two rows share the same rank  
• "dma_id", "dma_name": geographic context; not strictly required but useful for inspection  

Typical conditions that will be useful  
1. WHERE "refresh_date" = (SELECT MAX("refresh_date") …)     → latest dataset pull  
2. WHERE "week"  = (SELECT MAX("week") …)                     → latest week  
3. WHERE "week" = DATEADD(year,‑1, <latest_week>)             → week exactly one year before  
4. WHERE "rank" = 1                                           → the single top rising term  
5. ORDER BY "percent_gain" DESC                               → tie‑breaker in case ranks repeat  
6. LIMIT 20                                                   → preview rows only (as asked)  

Below are ten graduated Snowflake SQL samples, each limited to 20 rows and wrapped in comments that explain the purpose.

```sql
--Description: 1) Preview the 20 most‑recent refresh dates in the rising‑terms table
SELECT DISTINCT "refresh_date"
FROM "GOOGLE_TRENDS"."GOOGLE_TRENDS"."TOP_RISING_TERMS"
ORDER BY "refresh_date" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 2) Show the 20 most‑recent weeks available (independent of refresh date)
SELECT DISTINCT "week"
FROM "GOOGLE_TRENDS"."GOOGLE_TRENDS"."TOP_RISING_TERMS"
ORDER BY "week" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 3) Inspect all rows belonging to the single most‑recent refresh date
SELECT *
FROM "GOOGLE_TRENDS"."GOOGLE_TRENDS"."TOP_RISING_TERMS"
WHERE "refresh_date" = (SELECT MAX("refresh_date") 
                         FROM "GOOGLE_TRENDS"."GOOGLE_TRENDS"."TOP_RISING_TERMS")
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 4) Inspect all rows belonging to the single most‑recent week
SELECT *
FROM "GOOGLE_TRENDS"."GOOGLE_TRENDS"."TOP_RISING_TERMS"
WHERE "week" = (SELECT MAX("week") 
                FROM "GOOGLE_TRENDS"."GOOGLE_TRENDS"."TOP_RISING_TERMS")
ORDER BY "rank"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 5) Surface the top‑ranked (rank=1) rising term for the most‑recent week
SELECT "week", "term", "rank", "percent_gain", "dma_name"
FROM "GOOGLE_TRENDS"."GOOGLE_TRENDS"."TOP_RISING_TERMS"
WHERE "week" = (SELECT MAX("week") 
                FROM "GOOGLE_TRENDS"."GOOGLE_TRENDS"."TOP_RISING_TERMS")
  AND "rank" = 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 6) Calculate the week exactly one year prior to the most‑recent week
--(preview only; no DATE arithmetic on unknown formats beyond DATEADD)
SELECT DATEADD(year, -1, 
       (SELECT MAX("week") 
        FROM "GOOGLE_TRENDS"."GOOGLE_TRENDS"."TOP_RISING_TERMS")) AS "week_minus_1yr";
```

```sql
--Description: 7) Preview all rows for the week that is exactly one year before the latest week
SELECT *
FROM "GOOGLE_TRENDS"."GOOGLE_TRENDS"."TOP_RISING_TERMS"
WHERE "week" = DATEADD(year, -1, 
              (SELECT MAX("week") 
               FROM "GOOGLE_TRENDS"."GOOGLE_TRENDS"."TOP_RISING_TERMS"))
ORDER BY "rank"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 8) Grab the single top‑ranked rising term (rank=1) one year prior to the latest week
SELECT "week", "term", "rank", "percent_gain", "dma_name"
FROM "GOOGLE_TRENDS"."GOOGLE_TRENDS"."TOP_RISING_TERMS"
WHERE "week" = DATEADD(year, -1, 
              (SELECT MAX("week") 
               FROM "GOOGLE_TRENDS"."GOOGLE_TRENDS"."TOP_RISING_TERMS"))
  AND "rank" = 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 9) If multiple rows share rank=1, pick the one with the greatest percent_gain
SELECT "week", "term", "rank", "percent_gain", "dma_name"
FROM "GOOGLE_TRENDS"."GOOGLE_TRENDS"."TOP_RISING_TERMS"
WHERE "week" = DATEADD(year, -1, 
              (SELECT MAX("week") 
               FROM "GOOGLE_TRENDS"."GOOGLE_TRENDS"."TOP_RISING_TERMS"))
ORDER BY "rank" ASC, "percent_gain" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 10) Produce the final answer: one row containing the top term,
--                 its percent gain & geography for the target week
SELECT "term",
       "percent_gain",
       "dma_name",
       "dma_id",
       "week",
       "refresh_date"
FROM "GOOGLE_TRENDS"."GOOGLE_TRENDS"."TOP_RISING_TERMS"
WHERE "rank" = 1
  AND "week" = DATEADD(year, -1, 
              (SELECT MAX("week") 
               FROM "GOOGLE_TRENDS"."GOOGLE_TRENDS"."TOP_RISING_TERMS"))
ORDER BY "percent_gain" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

